course: everyday
lesson: 350
title: Chores you skipped
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Laziness rarely hurts only the lazy one. Ruby in Christchurch didn't feed her rabbit because it was raining, and told Mum she had. Mum saw the empty bowl and the hungry rabbit. Ruby cleaned the hutch on Saturday, said sorry, and fed him every day after. Pets can't tell you they're hungry, so you must remember for them.
explain: Homework went three ways. The first time Ruby didn't start it, the teacher never asked, and she thought she always could skip it. The second time she got four out of twenty in spellings and her planner came home to be signed. The third time the teacher called her parents in; there was a row at home and the console went away for a week.
explain: Ruby sulked — «It's not fair, everyone forgets sometimes!» — and wouldn't speak to Dad at tea. The next day she said sorry, and Dad helped her make a homework plan: same time every day, before screens. Getting away with it once had not made it safe; the bad mark and the lost console were consequences, not revenge.
explain: When Gran was away, Ruby forgot to water her plants and then didn't bother. Some died. Gran was sad, and Ruby felt it all week. She confessed it at church, felt the weight lift, and then did what the priest said: told Gran herself and bought her a new plant with her own money.
fact: A pet depends on you every day for food and water; it can't remind you.
fact: Hiding mess, like dirty socks under the bed, makes it grow, not vanish.
fact: Practice is often boring, but it is what makes you better.
fact: A bike left in the rain can rust, which costs more than putting it away.
fact: Skipping homework once without being asked does not mean it is safe to skip it again.
fact: A bad mark, a signed planner or a lost console are consequences meant to teach, not revenge.
fact: Feeling cross after a consequence is normal, but making a plan to do better is still your job.
fact: Toys left on the floor can trip someone, especially little children.
fact: A job you promised to do, like watering plants, matters to the person counting on you.
fact: Starting is usually the hardest part of a job.
fact: When one tired person clears up alone, the jobs take longer and the family loses time together, like reading before bed.
exercise: Why did Ruby not feed her rabbit? | It was raining and she didn't want to go out | Mum had already fed it before school | The rabbit wasn't hungry | She had run out of food
exercise: Why is forgetting a pet's food worse than forgetting your own? | The pet can't tell you or get food itself | Pets eat much less than people, so they hardly notice | Rabbits don't mind | It isn't worse at all
exercise: The first time Ruby skipped homework, the teacher didn't ask. What did Ruby wrongly think? | That she could always skip it | That homework wasn't needed | That the teacher was ill | That she had done it
exercise: What happened the second time? | Four out of twenty and a planner to sign at home | A gold star for being so honest about it | Nothing again | A day off school
exercise: What happened the third time? | Her parents were called in, a row at home, no console for a week | She got away with it again, because the teacher forgot to ask for it | The teacher did it for her | She was moved up a class
exercise: Why did Ruby sulk after losing the console? | She felt it was unfair | She was tired after school | She didn't like the console | Dad had asked her to sulk
exercise: What helped Ruby after the sulk? | Saying sorry and making a homework plan with Dad | Hiding her planner so nobody has to sign it again | Sulking for a week | Doing homework only on Fridays
exercise: A boy hides dirty socks under his bed instead of tidying. What happens? | The room smells and Mum finds them | The socks clean themselves under the bed | Nobody ever notices | The socks vanish
exercise: A girl leaves her bike in the rain all week. Why does the chain squeak? | It has gone rusty | Bikes squeak in summer | Someone else rode it | The tyres are flat
exercise: A boy doesn't help clear the table, so Mum, very tired, does it all. What did they lose? | Time together, like reading before bed | The TV, because it was switched off | Nothing at all | The dinner plates
exercise: Ruby's Gran's plants died while she was away. Who was let down? | Gran, who counted on her | Nobody, plants die anyway | Only the plants' pot, which cracked | The garden centre that sold them
exercise: After confessing about the plants, what did Ruby still need to do? | Tell Gran herself and replace a plant | Buy the priest a plant for the church instead | Nothing, confession was enough | Water plastic plants
exercise: A boy's toys are on the floor and his little brother trips and hurts his chin. What's the lesson? | Your mess can hurt someone else | Little brothers should look down | Toys belong on the floor where kids play | It's the brother's fault for not looking
exercise: A boy doesn't practise his recorder and plays badly at the concert. What would have saved the embarrassment? | Practising a little each day before | Playing louder so nobody hears mistakes | Not turning up | Blaming the recorder
exercise: What is usually the hardest part of a job like homework? | Starting it | Finishing it | Handing it in | Choosing a pen
test: Mum: «Rabbit's bowl is bone dry. You said you fed him». What's the honest answer? | «I didn't, it was raining. Sorry, I'll do it now» | «He must've eaten it really fast, he was starving» | «The rain washed it away» | «Dad fed him»
test: «didn't do my homework n miss never asked, so I'm not doing it again». What's true? | Next time she may ask, and it's still not learned | Fine, if the teacher never asks for it again | Homework doesn't count | Only tests count
test: Dad, angry: «Mrs Grant wants to see us. Again. Hand over the console». Ruby: «NOT FAIR!» What does Dad mean? | It's a consequence to help her change, not revenge | He wants to play it himself while she's at school | He's punishing her forever | He's joking
test: Mum's text: «room STINKS. what is under your bed??» What's the fix? | Own up, take the socks to the wash and tidy properly | Spray perfume all round the room and shut the window | Say it's the dog | Shut the door
test: Gran, back from holiday: «Oh, my geraniums...» What's the right thing? | Say sorry and buy her a new plant with her own money | Say they were old and dying anyway, it wasn't the watering | Say it was too hot | Say nothing
test: Brother crying with a bumped chin, tripped on Lego. What should the older one do? | Say sorry and tidy up before bed from now on | Say Dad should look where he walks in the dark | Say the Lego isn't his | Laugh
test: Kid: «skipped my spellings twice and still got 10, so practice is pointless». What's true? | Being lucky twice doesn't mean the words are learned | He's a natural speller, so practising would only waste his time | Spelling tests are easy | Practice really is pointless
test: Teacher, after the concert: «I could tell you hadn't practised». What's the best answer? | «Sorry, I'll practise more for next time» | «The recorder was broken, it squeaks on its own» | «Everyone was out of tune» | «It's too hard»
test: Dad: «Bike's rusty. Left it out again?» What fixes it? | Say sorry and oil it together, and put it away | Buy a new bike, since the old one is rusty now | Say rain is the problem | Say nothing
test: Which is TRUE about chores you skip? | Someone else usually has to do them or gets hurt | They matter only to grown-ups, who like things tidy | Nobody ever notices | They disappear by themselves
test: «just pretend you're asleep, Mum'll unpack the shopping herself». What's true? | Faking sleep is still a lie, and Mum does it alone | It's not lying if you close your eyes and say nothing at all | It's fine, she likes unpacking | It's fine on weekends
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the omission of homework on an occasion on which it was not checked. | The absence of enquiry on one occasion affords no assurance concerning subsequent occasions | The absence of enquiry establishes that homework is optional | Homework not checked need never be completed | The omission is of no consequence to the pupil's learning
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pet animal requires daily attention to its food and water. | It is wholly dependent upon its keeper and cannot obtain them itself | It requires food only weekly and suffers no harm from an occasional omission | It is capable of feeding itself | It suffers no harm from neglect
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the withdrawal of a games console following repeated failure to complete homework? | It is a consequence intended to promote improvement and not an act of revenge | It is an act of revenge by the parents | It is a permanent confiscation | It relieves the pupil of the duty to complete homework
exam: Determine what remains to be done by a child who has confessed neglecting the plants of a grandparent. | She ought to inform the grandparent and replace a plant at her own expense | Nothing further is required | She ought to conceal the matter | She ought to purchase a plant for the church
